course: dictionary
lesson: 463
title: Armour: a word with many coats
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Armour is a strong covering that protects something from harm. Soldiers and knights wore it long ago in battle. «Their best fighters were knights, rich horsemen trained from boyhood, who wore heavy armour» tells you that armour was heavy and worn to keep the body safe.
explain: The word also works in other ways. «Insects wear their skeleton outside like armour» means an insect's hard outer skeleton protects it, a bit like a metal suit. The clue is the word «like»: the writer is comparing the two, so armour can describe something that is not metal at all.
explain: Animals can have armour too. «some wear armour of quills» tells you that an animal is protected by spiky quills. Find the clue words «wear» and «of quills», then ask: what covers and protects the body?
explain: Some armour is only a picture. A «chink in the armour» means a weak spot where someone could be hurt. This phrase is not about real metal, so the words around it tell you what it means.
fact: Armour is a strong covering that protects the body or a vehicle.
fact: «Armour» can mean metal or leather covering worn by soldiers and knights in the past.
fact: «Armour» can also mean the hard shell or scales that protect an animal, such as an armadillo or a tortoise.
fact: A «suit of armour» is a full set of armour.
fact: In British English it is spelled armour; in American English it is armor.
exercise: Knights wore «heavy armour» into battle. What was it for? | *To protect the body in battle | To keep the knight's horse fed | To show the knight was late | To make the knight run faster in races
exercise: «Insects wear their skeleton outside like armour.» Which word tells you the skeleton is being compared to armour? | outside | *like | wear | skeleton
exercise: Which animal has natural armour, a hard shell made of plates? | a horse | *an armadillo | a dolphin | a rabbit
test: «a chink in the armour» means | *a weak spot where someone could be hurt | a prize for winning a fight | a hole made by a sword | a shiny piece of metal
test: Which spelling is used in British English? | *armour | armor | armoure | amour
exam: In the sentence «some wear armour of quills», what protects the animal? | a hard shell | *spiky quills | a metal helmet | soft fur
